here's a map of the uh  the part that we're interested in a  small part of a very big country and i  guess we're going to get fairly familiar  with this map in the weeks to come the  military logic of it is pretty clear the  russians would like to draw effectively  a military line pushing south from  kharkiv to meet up going north from  marriott to produce a sort of straight  line between kharkiv and mario paul and  isolate that  salient which is to the east of that  line and we've marked on the map here  all the places where intensive fighting  has been recorded over the last uh 24  hours so there's been intensive fighting  in chuhev in isiam in siverio dionyez in  popasna and in marinka and those are all  the areas where the ukrainians are  trying to hold the russian advance  because what the russians want to do  draw this north-south line and then push  from the areas they already hold from  the east towards the west to meet up  with that line now  that sounds pretty logical if you say it  quickly however there are some uh  caveats to all of that and it's not as  never as simple as it as it always  sounds first of all the distances are  pretty big 300 miles from north to south  so there's a lot of offensive to conduct  here secondly  i think the russians have got off to a  rolling start it doesn't seem to me as  if they've got off to what would not you  normally expect is a sort of hammer blow  start get get everything in place and  then really go  i think probably i'm guessing under  pressure from the kremlin they've got  off to a rolling start because they're  under pressure to produce something  pretty quickly and that's why the  ukrainians have been quite successful  certainly over the last day and a half  at holding these russian pushes  east to west in the way that they have  second problem is that the russians have  been counter-attacked the ukrainians are  not just holding them so far but they're  counter-attacking particularly around  southeast of kharkiv between kharkiv and  tuna here  and north of kharkiv there's a direct  line you can't see it on this map  between kharkiv and belgarot in russia  which is where most of the supplies are  coming from and the ukrainians are  attacking those communication lines to  try to prevent material getting through  and then finally of course  there is mariupol  in the south  in truth the russians have mario paul  they control it they control the whole  city  but there at the avistar steel works  there seem to be a thousand fifteen  hundred members of the ukrainian armed  forces who will not surrender they seem  to be in the tunnels and the bomb  shelters and the caverns underneath the  steel works which are pretty  considerable  and so those elements of the azo brigade  the 56 mechanized infantry the tenth  assault brigade  however many of them are left are under  there  and they seem to have decided that  nothing remains but to fight  and there is a military logic to that  because as long as they refuse to  surrender although their fate is now  fixed as long as they refuse to  surrender they are tying up russian  troops who otherwise would be going  north to try to build this line between  mario paul and karkov so there is a  military logic but of course there is  also a political and moral logic to all  of this  because they are now the epitome they  are i  iconic of the sort of conflict that this  has become this david and goliath  struggle so in saying what they say  it really matters to kiev and probably  to the rest of the world  that nothing remains but to fight
 

